title: Two outputs
check: behaves
mode: vhdl
chapter: Arithmetic
---- intro
Time to make a circuit do arithmetic. Adding two single bits gives 0, 1 or 2. Written in binary, 2 is **10**, so the answer needs two outputs: **sum** (the right digit) and **carry** (the left one). This is a **half adder**.

| a | b | carry | sum |
|---|---|-------|-----|
| 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 0 | 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 |

Look at the columns: `sum` is XOR, and `carry` is AND. Each output gets its own line. The order of the lines does not matter. Unlike a program, a circuit is not run line by line: all of its gates are there at the same time, working at once.
---- task
Build the half adder: `sum` is `a` XOR `b`, and `carry` is `a` AND `b`.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity half_adder is
  port(
    a     : in  std_logic;
    b     : in  std_logic;
    sum   : out std_logic;
    carry : out std_logic
  );
end half_adder;

architecture rtl of half_adder is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity half_adder is
  port(
    a     : in  std_logic;
    b     : in  std_logic;
    sum   : out std_logic;
    carry : out std_logic
  );
end half_adder;

architecture rtl of half_adder is
begin
    sum   <= a xor b;
    carry <= a and b;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Two lines, one for each output. The XOR gate's word is `xor`.
---- hint2
`sum <= a xor b;` and `carry <= a and b;`
---- solution
`sum <= a xor b;` and `carry <= a and b;`. 1 + 1 gives `carry` 1 and `sum` 0: binary 10, which is 2.
---- done
Swap the two lines round and nothing changes: they describe two gates that exist side by side. That is the biggest difference between VHDL and a programming language.
